      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The connected reduced alternating diagram and either checkerboard color.\n\n[F1] Connectedness, reducedness and alternation concern the finite diagram. Its checkerboard has a finite disk-and-band presentation. ([[def-reduced-alternating-link-diagram]], [[lem-alternating-checkerboard-normal-pairing-is-definite]])\n\n[F2] A connected reduced alternating diagram with crossings has a finite splitting tree with diagrammatically prime reduced alternating leaves. Each parent checkerboard is an explicit geometric boundary sum of its children, constructed in projection-disk product balls. ([[lem-reduced-alternating-diagram-has-finite-diagrammatic-prime-splitting]])\n\n[F3] The statement's zero- and one-suture disk property, and geometric essentiality, hold at every leaf of that tree. ([[lem-prime-reduced-alternating-checkerboards-are-essential]])\n\n[F4] The zero- and one-suture disk property is preserved by boundary sum of two sutured manifolds along disks each bisected by one suture arc. ([[lem-sutured-boundary-sum-preserves-disk-essentiality]])\n\n[F5] Geometric incompressibility together with absence of a one-suture disk in the cut-open exterior implies exterior boundary-incompressibility. ([[lem-exterior-boundary-compression-gives-one-suture-cap]])\n\n\n\n1.1 Suppose first that the diagram has crossings. Use [F2] to obtain its finite splitting tree, retaining the specified color at every node. Each node surface comes with the standard capping disks and link arcs in the product balls used in its split, so no comparison of abstract prime decompositions is needed. [given, F1, F2, construct]\n\n2.1 For each such surface boundary sum, its complementary sutured manifold is the boundary sum in [F4]. This can be checked in a boundary-arc chart: take the surface locally as $\\{z=0,y\\ge0\\}$, with boundary on the $x$-axis, and its interval thickening locally as $\\{y\\ge0,|z|\\le\\varepsilon\\}$. Its vertical boundary is $y=0$, and the link is the arc $z=0$ in that boundary. Choose a small disk there bisected by this arc. Gluing the two complementary manifolds along these disks removes the two marked link arcs and joins their exterior portions at the endpoints, while the two middle surfaces are joined by the boundary-sum strip. The coordinates round to smooth collars; away from them nothing changes. In the projection-product construction in [F2] the marked disks are precisely the complementary disks of the sphere cut along the surface strip. Thus the cores of the boundary annuli splice as in [F4], and this identification matches the actual diagrammatic parent, not just an unmarked abstract manifold. [F2, F4, step 1.1, construct]\n\n3.1 Apply [F3] at all leaves and [F4] at their parent nodes, using step 2.1. Continuing upward through the finite tree proves the stronger disk property at its root. Formally, induction on the number of vertices of a finite subtree suffices: the single-vertex case is [F3], and at a nonleaf root the two smaller subtrees give the hypotheses for [F4]. This proves the property for either checkerboard when crossings are present. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, construct]\n\n4.1 For a crossingless connected diagram, $F$ is a disk, and the cut-open exterior is a ball whose suture is one circle dividing its boundary into two disks. A circle bounding a proper disk in this ball cannot meet that suture exactly once, because a closed curve crosses a separating circle an even number of times. If it misses the suture, it lies in one of the two complementary disks and bounds a disk there. This is the same stronger disk property. All choices of annular cores give the same result: isotope the cores within their annuli and extend over a boundary collar to transport both the proper disks and their boundary disks. [given, F1, step 3.1, construct]\n\n5.1 To deduce geometric incompressibility, lift a hypothetical compressing disk to $N$. Its boundary is one lift of a simple closed curve $\\gamma$ in $F$ and misses $s$. By steps 3.1–3.2 it bounds a disk on $\\partial N\\setminus s$. Push this disk out of the half-annular collars into the normal double cover of $F$, keeping its boundary fixed. It projects to an embedded disk in $F$: the disk and its deck translate have disjoint boundaries, and therefore are disjoint or nested unless their union is a sphere component. Each component of the normal double cover has nonempty boundary, excluding the sphere alternative. Strict nesting is incompatible with the deck involution, which would reverse that strict inclusion. Hence the disks are disjoint, so the projection is injective and bounds $\\gamma$. The original disk was therefore not a compression. [step 3.1, step 4.1, construct]\n\n6.1 By [F5], an exterior boundary compression would now yield a one-suture disk in $N$, excluded by steps 3.1–3.2. Thus both checkerboards are geometrically incompressible and boundary-incompressible, including for diagrams which split nontrivially in [F2]. [F2, F5, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, construct] ∎"
